1. Introduction 
The Telecom Microservices System aims to enhance customer experience and streamline 
operations related to service provisioning, billing, and support in the telecom industry 
through a robust microservices architecture. 
2. Objectives 
• Improve customer interaction and satisfaction through self-service capabilities. 
• Streamline service provisioning and management processes. 
• Enhance billing accuracy and provide transparent billing information. 
• Enable efficient handling of customer support requests. 
3. Scope 
This document outlines ten key use cases relevant to the telecom domain that will be 
implemented using a microservices architecture. Each use case includes detailed descriptions 
and workflows. 
4. Use Cases 
Use Case 1: User Registration 
• Description: New users can create an account on the telecom platform. 
• Actors: User, Registration Service 
• Preconditions: The user provides valid personal information. 
• Postconditions: A new user account is created, and the user receives confirmation. 
• Main Flow: 
1. User navigates to the registration page. 
2. User fills out the registration form with required information (name, email, 
phone number, etc.). 
3. Registration Service validates the information (checks for duplicates). 
4. If valid, the service creates a new user record in the database. 
5. The service sends a confirmation email or SMS to the user. 
Use Case 2: Plan Search 
• Description: Users can search for available telecom plans based on their needs. 
• Actors: User, Plan Search Service 
• Preconditions: The user is logged in or browsing as a guest. 
• Postconditions: The user receives a list of available telecom plans. 
• Main Flow: 
1. User accesses the plan search page. 
2. User enters search criteria (data limits, price range, type of plan). 
3. Plan Search Service retrieves relevant plans from the database. 
4. The service formats the plan information (features, pricing) for display. 
5. The user views the list of available telecom plans on the screen. 
Use Case 3: Subscribe to a Plan 
• Description: Users can subscribe to a selected telecom plan. 
• Actors: User, Subscription Service, Plan Service 
• Preconditions: The user has selected a telecom plan. 
• Postconditions: The user is successfully subscribed to the selected plan. 
• Main Flow: 
1. User selects a plan from the search results. 
2. Subscription Service retrieves plan details and prompts the user for 
confirmation. 
3. User confirms the subscription and provides any required personal 
information. 
4. The Subscription Service processes the subscription and updates the user’s 
account. 
5. The user receives a confirmation email or SMS regarding the subscription. 
Use Case 4: Manage Account 
• Description: Users can view and manage their account information. 
• Actors: User, Account Management Service 
• Preconditions: The user is logged into their account. 
• Postconditions: The user’s account information is updated. 
• Main Flow: 
1. User logs into their account and navigates to the "My Account" section. 
2. Account Management Service retrieves the user’s account details. 
3. User can view and edit personal information (address, contact numbers). 
4. User submits changes to update their information. 
5. The service confirms the updates and notifies the user via email or SMS. 
Use Case 5: Billing and Payment 
• Description: Users can view their bills and make payments for services. 
• Actors: User, Billing Service, Payment Service 
• Preconditions: The user has an active account with outstanding bills. 
• Postconditions: The user’s payment is processed successfully. 
• Main Flow: 
1. User navigates to the billing section of their account. 
2. Billing Service retrieves the user’s current bill details. 
3. User selects a payment method and enters payment information. 
4. Payment Service processes the payment and updates the billing records. 
5. The user receives a confirmation of payment via email or SMS. 
Use Case 6: View Usage History 
• Description: Users can view their usage history for calls, messages, and data. 
• Actors: User, Usage Tracking Service 
• Preconditions: The user is logged into their account. 
• Postconditions: The user views a detailed history of their usage. 
• Main Flow: 
1. User logs into their account and navigates to the "Usage History" section. 
2. Usage Tracking Service retrieves the user’s usage data from the database. 
3. The service displays a breakdown of call, message, and data usage. 
4. User can filter the history by date range or type of usage. 
5. The user can download or print the usage report if needed. 
Use Case 7: Customer Support Request 
• Description: Users can submit support requests for assistance. 
• Actors: User, Support Service 
• Preconditions: The user must be logged in or have an active account. 
• Postconditions: The support request is logged and acknowledged. 
• Main Flow: 
1. User navigates to the customer support section of the app or website. 
2. User fills out a support request form detailing the issue. 
3. Support Service logs the request in the database. 
4. The service assigns a support ticket and sends an acknowledgment to the user. 
5. A support representative contacts the user to resolve the issue. 
Use Case 8: Plan Upgrade/Downgrade 
• Description: Users can upgrade or downgrade their current telecom plan. 
• Actors: User, Plan Management Service 
• Preconditions: The user is logged into their account with an active plan. 
• Postconditions: The user’s plan is updated as per the request. 
• Main Flow: 
1. User logs into their account and navigates to the "Manage Plans" section. 
2. Plan Management Service retrieves the user’s current plan details. 
3. User selects the option to upgrade or downgrade their plan. 
4. The service presents available options and their details for selection. 
5. The user confirms the change, and the service updates the account 
accordingly. 
Use Case 9: View Service Outages 
• Description: Users can view information about service outages in their area. 
• Actors: User, Outage Notification Service 
• Preconditions: The user is logged in or browsing as a guest. 
• Postconditions: The user is informed about current or upcoming outages. 
• Main Flow: 
1. User navigates to the service outage page. 
2. Outage Notification Service retrieves outage data based on the user's location. 
3. The service displays information about current outages and estimated 
resolution times. 
4. User can subscribe to alerts for updates on the outage. 
5. The service confirms the subscription for outage notifications. 
Use Case 10: Loyalty Program Management 
• Description: Users can view and manage their participation in loyalty programs. 
• Actors: User, Loyalty Program Service 
• Preconditions: The user is logged into their account. 
• Postconditions: The user’s loyalty program status is updated or viewed. 
• Main Flow: 
1. User logs into their account and navigates to the "Loyalty Program" section. 
2. Loyalty Program Service retrieves the user’s loyalty points and status. 
3. User can view available rewards and redeem points if eligible. 
4. The service processes any redemptions and updates the loyalty points balance. 
5. The user receives confirmation of the redemption and updated points. 
5. Non-Functional Requirements 
Overview 
This section outlines the non-functional requirements for this application utilizing a 
microservices architecture implemented with Spring Cloud, MySQL, OAuth2, and JWT. 
These requirements are essential for ensuring that the application meets performance, 
security, and operational standards. 
5.1. Scalability 
• Horizontal Scaling: Each microservice must be designed to scale horizontally by 
deploying multiple instances based on demand. Load balancing must be 
implemented to distribute traffic evenly. 
• Service Discovery: Utilize Spring Cloud Netflix Eureka for dynamic service 
discovery, enabling microservices to find and communicate with each other 
efficiently. 
5.2. Performance 
• Response Time: 
o The system should respond to user requests within 200 milliseconds for 
95% of operations. 
o For batch processing tasks (e.g., bulk transactions), the maximum 
acceptable response time should not exceed 2 seconds. 
• Throughput: Each microservice should handle a minimum of 100 transactions 
per second under normal load conditions. 
5.3. Security 
• Data Encryption: 
o Sensitive data (e.g., user credentials, personal information) must be 
encrypted in MySQL using AES-256 encryption. 
o All communication between microservices must be encrypted using TLS 
(Transport Layer Security). 
• Authentication and Authorization: 
o Implement OAuth2 for user authentication, utilizing JWTs (JSON Web 
Tokens) for secure token-based access. 
o Tokens must have an expiration time of 15 minutes and should be 
refreshable without re-authentication for active sessions. 
5.4. Availability 
• Uptime Requirement: The system must achieve 99.9% availability, with 
scheduled maintenance windows communicated in advance to users. 
• Failover Mechanisms: Implement circuit breaker patterns using Spring Cloud 
Resilience4j to prevent cascading failures and ensure resilience under load. 
5.5. Compliance 
• Data Protection: Ensure compliance with relevant data protection regulations 
such as GDPR and PCI DSS. 
• Audit Logging: Maintain detailed logs of user activities, access events, and 
system changes for audit purposes. Logs should be retained for a minimum of 6 
months. 
5.6. Reliability 
• Error Handling: Each microservice should have robust error handling and return 
appropriate HTTP status codes (e.g., 4xx for client errors, 5xx for server errors). 
• Data Consistency: Implement eventual consistency for distributed transactions 
using patterns like Saga or CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation). 
5.7. Maintainability 
• Code Modularity: Each microservice must be modular and follow the Single 
Responsibility Principle, making it easier to update and deploy independently. 
• Configuration: Each microservice must be configure all external properties using 
Spring Cloud Config server. 
• CI/CD Pipeline: Establish a Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment 
(CI/CD) pipeline using tools like Jenkins or GitLab CI for automated testing and 
deployment of microservices. 
5.8. Usability 
• API Documentation: Provide comprehensive API documentation using 
Swagger/OpenAPI to facilitate ease of use for developers integrating with the 
microservices. 
• Error Messages: Implement user-friendly error messages and codes to guide 
users in troubleshooting issues. 
5.9. Monitoring and Logging 
• Centralized Logging: Use a centralized logging solution (e.g., ELK Stack - 
Elasticsearch, Logstash, Kibana) for collecting and analyzing logs from all 
microservices. 
• Performance Monitoring: Implement monitoring tools (e.g., Spring Boot 
Actuator, Prometheus, Grafana) to track system performance metrics, service 
health, and alert for anomalies. 
5.10. Interoperability 
• Standard Protocols: Ensure that all microservices communicate using RESTful 
APIs or gRPC, adhering to standard HTTP methods and response formats (JSON). 
• API Gateway: Utilize Spring Cloud Gateway to provide a single entry point for 
all microservices, enabling routing, load balancing, and additional security 
measures. 
6. Conclusion 
The Telecom Microservices System aims to enhance operational efficiency and user 
experience through the outlined use cases. This architecture will facilitate rapid deployment, 
scalability, and an improved user experience. 
